'' A DIM SHARED IS ONE MODULE'S OBJECT, and LLVM's 'internal' is what says so -- C's
'' 'static', which is what fbc writes. Emitted external, two modules that each declared
'' one were 'lld-link: error: duplicate symbol: e' where fbc compiles both; the same for
'' a 'private sub' holding a 'static'.
''
'' AND WHAT IS VISIBLE BUT NOT YET GIVEN A VALUE IS 'common' -- C's tentative
'' definition, which the linker MERGES rather than refusing. See the source for fbc's
'' measured table of all nine shapes.
''
'' NOT ASSERTED HERE, because the harness compiles one file: the multi-module programs.
'' Those were run against fbc by hand and their output matched byte for byte.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/shared-linkage.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' NOBODY ELSE'S: a shared variable, initialised or not, and a static local.
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"sh" = internal global
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"noinit" = internal global
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"stsh" = internal global
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"P$sl" = internal global
'' VISIBLE AND UNVALUED, so the linker merges the copies. 'common shared' is always
'' here: it cannot be initialized at all.
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"CM$" = common global i32 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"_ZN1V2A$E" = common global i32 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"E$" = common global i32 0
'' VISIBLE AND VALUED -- a definition, and the same two shapes as the line above. It is
'' whether an initializer was WRITTEN that separates them.
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"_ZN1V2B$E" = global i32 9
'' EXPECT-OUT: @"EI$" = global i32 5
'' Marking either of those two 'common' would be an LLVM error, since a common global
'' must be zero-initialised; marking any of the six above 'internal' is the duplicate
'' symbol pointing the other way, a reference from another module that cannot resolve.
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"CM$" = internal
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"E$" = internal
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"EI$" = common
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"_ZN1V2B$E" = common
